'''unition''' (ū-nish′o̤n), ''n.'' [< ML. ''unitio(n-)'', a uniting, < LL. ''unire'', unite: see ''unite''<sup>1</sup>.] The act of uniting, or the state of being united; junction; union. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|As long as any different substance keeps off the ''unition'', hope not to cure the wound. {{right|''Wiseman'', Surgery, v. 1.}}

The precise and total meaning of Christianity . . . is that it affirms the perfect ''unition'' of the Divine and human natures in Christ. {{right|''H. James'', Subs. and Shad., p. 242.}}}}

'''unitism''' (ū′ni-tizm), ''n.'' [< ''unit'' + ''-ism.''] Same as ''monism'', 1.

'''unitive''' (ū′ni-tiv), ''a.'' [< ''unite''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ive.''] Having the power of uniting; causing or tending to unite; producing or promoting union; harmonizing.

{{smaller block|There is a degree of meditation so exalted that it changes the very name, and is called contemplation; and it is in the ''unitive'' way of religion—that is, it consists in unions and adherences to God.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 71.}}
A ''unitive'' power.
{{right|''J. H. Newman'', Development of Christ. Doct., i. 33.}}}}

'''unitively''' (ū′ni-tiv-li), ''adv.'' In a unitive or united manner. ''Cudworth.'' [Rare.]

