'''4.''' The state of being united or combined in one; especially, union as connected parts of a complex whole: as, the national ''unity'' of the separate states.
{{smaller block|England had hardly as yet [829] realized the need of national ''unity'', and outside the king’s council chamber there can have been few who understood the need of union between the nations of Christendom.
{{right|''J. R. Green'', Conq. of Eng., ii.}}}}

'''5.''' Harmony or accord in sentiments, affection, action, etc.; concord.
{{smaller block|How good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in ''unity''!
{{right|Ps. cxxxiii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Unity'', secrecy, decision, are the qualities which military arrangements require.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hallam’s Const. Hist.}}}}

'''6.''' Sameness of character or effect; agreement; coincidence.
{{smaller block|There is such ''unity'' in the proofs.
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., v. 2. 35.}}}}

